+# PROBE - WITH RECURSIVE round collapser + folded consolidation
+
+Feature branch `feature/sqlite-recursive-probe`. Two questions measured on the
+same fixtures the PERF-REPORT appendix banked: DAG 960k and CYC 960k s7
+(`gen_multi_cyclic(6,160000,{0,7})`, nodes=960002). Every step < 10 s.
+
+- **Q1** - does a recursive CTE collapse the retraction round loop into one VM entry?
+- **Q2** - does an append-only delta + incremental fold cut b-tree writes?
+
+## Contents
+
+- [The one-line answer](#the-one-line-answer)
+- [Q1 · what a recursive CTE can and cannot own](#q1--what-a-recursive-cte-can-and-cannot-own)
+- [Q1 · wall + statement table](#q1--wall--statement-table)
+- [Q2 · folded consolidation](#q2--folded-consolidation)
+- [Did the representation gap move?](#did-the-representation-gap-move)
+- [Measure: b-tree write volume](#measure-b-tree-write-volume)
+- [Receipts and reproduce](#receipts-and-reproduce)
+
+## The one-line answer
+
+A recursive CTE owns the traversal (cone, rederive, reach) in one VM entry each,
+and the unrounded distinct-reach form also wins on wall (RS: 3 stmts, 1133 ms vs
+dred-loop 53/1750, signed-delta 27/1673). The ROUND column is the one thing a
+recursive CTE cannot own: SQLite rejects a second self-reference, and rounding
+breaks the UNION-distinct termination a cycle depends on. So the round loop stays
+in Rust; the CTE absorbs the walk.
+
+## Q1 · what a recursive CTE can and cannot own
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    subgraph CTE_OWNS["recursive CTE owns (single self-ref, UNION distinct)"]
+        A[over-delete cone] 
+        B[rederive set]
+        C[reach from roots]
+    end
+    subgraph LOOP_OWNS["stays in Rust (round loop)"]
+        D["per-round delta staging"]
+        E["refcount / seen fold (needs accumulated set)"]
+        F["weight publish"]
+    end
+    CTE_OWNS --> LOOP_OWNS
+```
+
+Empirical walls, verified on sqlite3 3.43 (libsqlite3 bundled):
+
+| form | result |
+|---|---|
+| `WITH RECURSIVE r(d,k) AS (SELECT 0,0 UNION SELECT d+1,e.c ...)` over a cycle | **never terminates** (round column defeats UNION-distinct; (d,k) pairs are all distinct) |
+| adding the guard `... WHERE e.c NOT IN (SELECT k FROM r)` | **rejected**: `multiple recursive references: r` |
+| distinct reach `r(k)` with no round column over a cycle | terminates (0,1,2,3) |
+
+Why the ROUND cannot ride as a CTE column: the recursive term may reference the
+CTE name exactly once (so the accumulated-set guard is impossible), and a round
+column turns every cycle into fresh rows forever. The constraint leaves exactly
+the UNROUNDED distinct walk, which is why the pieces that must see the accumulated
+alive set (delta staging, refcount fold) live outside the CTE, in the Rust round
+loop.
+
+Where each variant does its work (cone, rederive, reach, round loop):
+
+| form | over-delete cone | rederive | reach from roots | round loop |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| `retract_dred` (loop) | 1 loop | 1 loop | - | Rust per round |
+| `retract_dred_cte` | 1 CTE | 1 CTE | - | none |
+| `retract_signed_delta` (loop) | - | - | 1 loop | Rust per round |
+| `retract_signed_delta_cte` (RS) | - | - | 1 CTE | none |
+
+RS and `retract_signed_delta` are the same survivor set: the per-round
+"not already alive" guard only stops re-emission, it does not change the
+least-fixpoint result. RS just computes that fixpoint in one distinct walk.
+
+## Q1 · wall + statement table
+
+Same 960k fixtures. `stmts` = SQL statement dispatches in the retract only
+(setup excluded). `new-page MB` = persistent b-tree page growth (see
+[Measure](#measure-b-tree-write-volume)).
+
+### DAG 960k (nodes=960002)
+
+| variant | ms | stmts | survivors | oracle-equal | new-page MB |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|:---:|---:|
+| dred-loop | 1750.2 | 53 | 800002 | yes | 0.00 |
+| dred-cte | 2540.8 | 6 | 800002 | yes | 0.00 |
+| signed-delta | 1672.6 | 27 | 800002 | yes | 19.56 |
+| **signed-delta-cte (RS)** | **1133.0** | **3** | 800002 | yes | 0.00 |
+| delta-fold (FC) | 1228.6 | 27 | 800002 | yes | 18.12 |
+| dd (banked) | 175.4 | 0 | 800002 | yes | - |
+
+### CYC 960k s7 (nodes=960002, 815240 survivors)
+
+| variant | ms | stmts | survivors | oracle-equal | new-page MB |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|:---:|---:|
+| dred-loop | 1908.0 | 53 | 815240 | yes | 0.00 |
+| dred-cte | 2682.2 | 6 | 815240 | yes | 0.00 |
+| signed-delta | 1872.9 | 27 | 815240 | yes | 19.75 |
+| **signed-delta-cte (RS)** | **1304.7** | **3** | 815240 | yes | 0.00 |
+| delta-fold (FC) | 1375.0 | 27 | 815240 | yes | 18.84 |
+| oracle (banked) | 379.4 | 0 | 815240 | ref | - |
+
+Banked cross-check: dred-loop 1753.4 ms / 53 stmts and signed-delta
+1669.7 ms / 27 stmts (PERF-REPORT appendix) reproduce here within noise
+(1750.2 / 53, 1672.6 / 27).
+
+Q1 read on the table:
+
+- Statement crossings collapse: dred 53 -> dred-cte 6 -> RS 3. signed-delta 27 -> RS 3.
+- RS also wins wall: 1133 ms beats dred-loop 1750, dred-cte 2541, signed-delta 1673.
+- dred-cte (6 stmts) is SLOWER than dred-loop (53 stmts) on this wide frontier,
+  per the banked shape rule: a recursive CTE re-walks its whole accumulated set
+  each iteration, so a wide frontier costs more per VM entry than the loop's
+  incremental per-round dispatch. RS beats both because it is one reach walk,
+  not the DRed over-delete cone + rederive pair.
+
+## Q2 · folded consolidation
+
+`retract_delta_fold` (FC) is `retract_signed_delta` with the refcount accumulator
+folded over time: `DELETE` then refill `cx_refcount` from the whole `cx_row` is
+replaced by an append-only accumulator that grows by `INSERT OR IGNORE` each round
+(the running reached set), and the per-round delta fold replaces any periodic
+GROUP BY/HAVING consolidation. No refill of the 960k corpus ever runs.
+
+| variant | statement count | new-page MB (DAG 960k) | survivors |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| signed-delta (refill) | 27 | 19.56 | 800002 |
+| delta-fold (FC) | 27 | 18.12 | 800002 |
+| delta | 0 | -1.44 | - |
+
+The b-tree growth FC removes is the ~160k dead rows the refill materialized into
+`cx_refcount` (160002 rows x ~9 B/row ~ 1.4 MB). The statement count is
+unchanged (27) because the refill was already one fused `DELETE+INSERT` dispatch;
+the saving is write volume, not crossings. `delta` stays append-only for the tick.
+
+## Did the representation gap move?
+
+For a single retraction, the delta/refcount scaffold RS drops buys nothing
+measurable: RS reaches the identical survivor set with fewer statements, less
+wall, and zero new b-tree pages. The scaffold preserves the incremental story
+(the per-round delta is the reuse seam for future ticks), but that seam is not
+exercised by the retract as written, which rebuilds state from scratch each call
+either way. So the gap moved for the single-shot case (RS is the recon floor),
+and the delta tables are currently overhead, not an answer RS lacks.
+
+## Measure: b-tree write volume
+
+`sqlite3_status(SQLITE_STATUS_PAGEWRITE)` is not compiled into the bundled
+libsqlite3: opcode 7 is the deprecated `PAGECACHE_SIZE` memory counter, not a page
+write counter (read from `sqlite3/sqlite3.c` status dispatch). So page WRITE
+counts are proxied by the checkpoint-truncated db+WAL byte delta bracketing the
+retract. That delta only counts NEW pages grown (in-place page rewrites do not
+change file size), so it understates total page writes; it is used as a relative,
+lower-bound proxy. `new-page MB` 0.00 for the dred/RS variants means pure in-place
+weight flips, no new leaf pages.
+
+## Receipts and reproduce
+
+- Byte-identical survivors vs oracle: `tests/agreement.rs` now carries RS
+  (`retract_signed_delta_cte`) and FC (`retract_delta_fold`) across the DAG +
+  cyclic + phantom-cycle matrix; `cargo test --test agreement` is green. Probe
+  `correct=yes` on every row above.
+- Statement counts per variant: the `stmts` column, from `stmt_counter` reset
+  around each measured retract.
+- Reproduce: `cargo run --release --example recursive_probe -- 6 160000 0` (DAG)
+  and `... 6 160000 7` (CYC). New methods: `cascade::retract_signed_delta_cte`,
+  `cascade::retract_delta_fold`, exposed on `RelStore`.
+
+| verify | command | result |
+|---|---|---|
+| agreement (RS + FC in matrix) | `cargo test --test agreement` | 4 passed |
+| cascade unit tests (RS + FC cycle fixtures) | `cargo test --lib cascade` | 9 passed |
+| bench, all steps < 10 s | `cargo run --release --example recursive_probe` | max ~2.7 s |
